package cajeta.net.http;

import cajeta.lang.String;

/**
 * Thrown by the streaming body decoder ([`BodyReader`](BodyReader.cajeta),
 * NET-7.4) when a `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` body is **syntactically
 * malformed** — a chunk-size line that is not a valid hexadecimal number,
 * a size line that overflows the chunk-length counter, a chunk whose
 * data is not followed by the mandated `CRLF`, or a trailer section that
 * is missing its terminating blank line. This is the
 * `InvalidChunkEncoding` failure the RFC 7230 §4.1 hardening posture
 * requires (and the taxonomy in
 * `test/net/golden/http/abuse.vectors` names): a conforming decoder
 * **rejects** a corrupt chunk stream rather than silently mis-framing
 * the body.
 *
 * The `position` field carries the 0-based byte offset (into the body
 * stream, counting from the first body byte fed to the reader) at which
 * the decoder noticed the problem, so a caller can point at the
 * offending byte — the same `(message, position)` shape
 * [`MalformedMessageException`](MalformedMessageException.cajeta) carries
 * for head parse failures.
 *
 * ## Hierarchy
 *
 * The spec ([`docs/Net.md`](../../../../../docs/Net.md))
 * places the HTTP parse failures (`MalformedMessage`, `HeadersTooLarge`,
 * `InvalidChunkEncoding`, `UnexpectedEof`) under `cajeta.net.NetException`
 * as plan item **NET-7.7**. That item has now landed: the four leaves
 * descend from the intermediate
 * [`HttpException`](HttpException.cajeta) → `cajeta.net.NetException`
 * root, so this is catchable as `HttpException` (every HTTP parse fault)
 * or `NetException` (every networking fault). (NET-7.4's placeholder
 * originally rooted this on `cajeta.error.RecoverableException` because
 * the NET-7.7 base had not been built; NET-7.7 is that one-line
 * reparent.) The NET-7.4 `(message, position)` constructor contract is
 * **unchanged** across the reparent — existing call sites and
 * `e.position`-reading catch blocks keep working byte-for-byte.
 *
 * ## The inherited `kind`
 *
 * As a `NetException`, every instance carries the cross-platform
 * [`kind`](../NetException.cajeta) ordinal. A malformed chunked body is
 * an *invalid-input* fault, not an errno-derived socket error, so it is
 * classified to [`NetException.KIND_INVALID`](../NetException.cajeta)
 * (12) — the whole HTTP family's bucket. The precise byte offset still
 * lives in `position`; `kind` is the coarse cross-subtype discriminant.
 */
public class InvalidChunkEncodingException extends HttpException {
    /**
     * Byte offset (0-based) into the body stream at which the decoder
     * rejected the chunk encoding. `-1` when the failure is not tied to
     * a single position.
     */
    public int64 position;

    public InvalidChunkEncodingException(String message, int64 position) {
        this.message = message;
        this.cause = 0;
        this.kind = 12;   // NetException.KIND_INVALID (via HttpException)
        this.position = position;
    }
}
